1
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:11,000
In 2004, on a forum for people who
write Sega Genesis games in BASIC, a
Venezuelan programmer named Tom
Maneiro asked a question.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,720
Can somebody suggest me a code for
simulate a bus horn?

3
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:18,050
Somebody answered.

4
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:19,720
And the answer became CrazyBus.

5
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:24,380
CrazyBus is a tech demo Tom wrote to
test his friend's BASIC compiler,
and his own sound driver.

6
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:26,080
You move a bus left and right.

7
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:27,060
You can honk the horn.

8
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:27,740
That's the game.

9
00:00:27,740 --> 00:00:31,233
No passengers, no destinations, no
way to lose, no ending.

10
00:00:31,233 --> 00:00:32,980
The screen just wraps around.

11
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:36,820
The buses are real Venezuelan and
Brazilian models, because of course
they are.

12
00:00:36,820 --> 00:00:39,560
And the whole game is in Spanish,
except the title, which is in
English.

13
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:41,120
The title got the least thought.

14
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:43,380
The music is what made it famous.

15
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,980
Tom fed random numbers into the
Genesis sound chip and let them
play.

16
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:52,940
The result sounds like a fax machine
having a panic attack.

17
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:54,700
And here's the detail nobody tells
you: it's not even random.

18
00:00:54,960 --> 00:00:56,660
It's a bad random number generator.

19
00:00:57,220 --> 00:01:00,895
So every time you turn the game on,
you get the exact same panic attack.

20
00:01:00,895 --> 00:01:02,120
Unless you mash buttons.

21
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:03,760
Then you get a different one.

22
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:06,140
Tom called that, bringing your own
entropy.

23
00:01:06,420 --> 00:01:09,660
For five years, CrazyBus sat on a
forum with about ten regulars.

24
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:13,940
Then ROM collectors swept it into
their full sets, thousands of games,
onto thousands of hard drives.

25
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:18,100
In 2009, somebody uploaded the title
screen to YouTube.

26
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:22,080
Two and a half million views later,
Tom found out his little demo was a
fever.

27
00:01:22,540 --> 00:01:24,968
His words, and I quote: Youtube is
scary.

28
00:01:24,968 --> 00:01:25,620
Scary shit.

29
00:01:26,140 --> 00:01:28,428
He rewrote the whole game in ten
days, just to troll the YouTubers.

30
00:01:28,428 --> 00:01:28,940
Nobody noticed.

31
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:32,160
Nobody had bothered to find out who
made it.

32
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:36,380
Then a scam company started
DMCA-striking every CrazyBus video,
claiming they owned it.

33
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:38,360
Tom, who actually owned it, couldn't
stop them.

34
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:41,640
His public response: I'm the sole
owner of the CrazyBus trademark.

35
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:43,440
Go copyright your ass, and your
mother's ass!

36
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:45,900
Bootleggers printed physical
cartridges without asking.

37
00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:46,900
He never got a penny.

38
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:58,980
Meanwhile the community did what
communities do: a Sega CD port with
Darude's Sandstorm and two Rick
Astley songs on it, an NES demake, a
PICO-8 version, and a real
championship in Brazil, where the
entire sport is holding right on the
D-pad for as long as you can.

39
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:03,240
In 2014, the Angry Video Game Nerd
opened a present on his Christmas
show.

40
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:05,380
Inside was a bootleg CrazyBus
cartridge.

41
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:07,500
Big Rigs is more exciting than this
shit, he said.

42
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:08,935
Four million people watched.

43
00:02:08,935 --> 00:02:10,600
Tom called it his lifetime
achievement.

44
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:13,820
Then he posted one last Happy New
Year in 2015, and vanished.

45
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:14,561
No merch.

46
00:02:14,561 --> 00:02:15,172
No podcast.

47
00:02:15,172 --> 00:02:15,840
No comeback.

48
00:02:16,060 --> 00:02:18,500
He made the worst game ever made, on
purpose, for himself.

49
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:20,780
And when the whole internet came for
it, he just let it go.

50
00:02:20,780 --> 00:02:22,000
The internet took the bus.

51
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:24,200
But the bus was never for us.

52
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:26,600
Some things you make because you
want to know if you can.

53
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:28,280
That's its own destination.
